1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:06,310
By the 1840s New York was the leading commercial city of the United States.
到19世纪40年代，纽约已是美国首屈一指的商业城市。

2
00:00:07,110 --> 00:00:29,310
It had long since outpaced Philadelphia as the largest city in the country, and even though Boston continued to be venerated as the cultural capital of the nation, its image had become somewhat languid and effete; it had not kept up with the implications of the newly industrialized economy, of a diversified ethnic population, or of the rapidly rising middle class.
纽约早已超过费城，成为全国最大的城市；波士顿尽管仍被尊为全国的文化之都，形象却已变得有些慵懒乏力、萎靡颓废；它没能适应新近工业化的经济、多元化的族裔人口以及迅速崛起的中产阶级所带来的种种影响。

3
00:00:30,110 --> 00:00:38,080
New York was the place where the “new” America was coming into being, so it is hardly surprising that the modern newspaper had its birth there.
纽约是“新”美国正在形成的地方，所以现代报纸诞生于此，也就不足为奇了。

4
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,900
The penny paper had found its first success in New York.
便士报最初就是在纽约取得成功的。

5
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:47,610
By the mid-1830s Ben Day’s Sun was drawing readers from all walks of life.
到19世纪30年代中期，本·戴的《太阳报》已经吸引着各行各业的读者。

6
00:00:48,410 --> 00:00:56,060
On the other hand the Sun was a skimpy sheet providing little more than minor diversions; few today would call it a newspaper at all.
另一方面，《太阳报》只是一份内容单薄的小报，提供的不过是些无关紧要的消遣；如今很少有人会认为它称得上是报纸。

7
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:05,060
Day himself was an editor of limited vision, and he did not possess the ability or the imagination to climb the slopes to loftier heights.
戴本人是个眼界有限的编辑，既没有能力，也没有想象力沿着山坡攀向更高处。

8
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:22,380
If real newspapers were to emerge from the public’s demand for more and better coverage, they would have to come from a youthful generation of editors for whom journalism was a totally absorbing profession, an exacting vocational ideal rather than a mere offshoot of job printing.
真正的报纸若要因公众对更多、更好报道的需求而诞生，就得出自年轻一代的编辑之手：对他们来说，新闻业是一项令人全身心投入的职业，是一种要求严苛的职业理想，而不只是零活印刷的一个分支。

9
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:35,190
By the 1840s two giants burst into the field, editors who would revolutionize journalism, would bring the newspaper into the modern age, and show how it could be influential in the national life.
到19世纪40年代，两位巨擘闯入了这一领域，这两位编辑后来彻底变革了新闻业，把报纸带入了现代，并展示了报纸可以怎样在国家生活中发挥影响力。

10
00:01:35,990 --> 00:01:43,060
These two giants, neither of whom has been treated kindly by history, were James Gordon Bennett and Horace Greeley.
这两位巨擘都没有得到历史的善待，他们就是詹姆斯·戈登·贝内特和霍勒斯·格里利。

11
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:50,060
Bennett founded his New York Herald in 1835, less than two years after the appearance of the Sun.
贝内特于1835年创办了《纽约先驱报》，距《太阳报》问世还不到两年。

12
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:54,100
Horace Greeley founded his Tribune in 1841.
霍勒斯·格里利于1841年创办了《论坛报》。

13
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:59,910
Bennett and Greeley were the most innovative editors in New York until after the Civil War.
直到南北战争结束以后，贝内特和格里利一直是纽约最具创新精神的编辑。

14
00:02:00,710 --> 00:02:06,470
Their newspapers were the leading American papers of the day, although for completely different reasons.
他们的报纸是当时美国数一数二的报纸，不过各自的原因完全不同。

15
00:02:07,270 --> 00:02:14,770
The two men despised each other, although not in the ways that newspaper editors had despised one another a few years before.
两人互相鄙视，不过与几年前报纸编辑之间互相鄙视的方式不同。

16
00:02:15,570 --> 00:02:18,990
Neither was a political hack shackled to a political party.
两人都不是受某个政党束缚的御用文人。

17
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:22,530
Greeley fancied himself a public intellectual.
格里利自命为公共知识分子。

18
00:02:23,330 --> 00:02:33,740
He had strong political views, and he wanted to run for office himself, but party factotum he could never be; he bristled with ideals and causes of his own devising.
他政治观点鲜明，也想亲自竞选公职，但永远不可能当政党的听差杂役；他满脑子都是自己构想出来的理想和主张。

19
00:02:34,540 --> 00:02:40,660
Officially he was a Whig (and later a Republican), but he seldom gave comfort to his chosen party.
名义上他是辉格党人（后来又成了共和党人），但他很少让自己选定的政党宽心。

20
00:02:41,460 --> 00:02:56,730
Bennett, on the other hand, had long since cut his political ties, and although his paper covered local and national politics fully and he went after politicians with hammer and tongs, Bennett was a cynic, a distruster of all settled values.
相比之下，贝内特早已斩断了自己的政治关系；尽管他的报纸对地方和全国政治报道得十分全面，他本人也对政客们猛烈抨击，但贝内特是个愤世嫉俗的人，不信任一切既定的价值观。

21
00:02:57,530 --> 00:03:03,380
He did not regard himself as an intellectual, although in fact he was better educated than Greeley.
他并不认为自己是知识分子，尽管实际上他受的教育比格里利更好。

22
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:06,940
He thought himself only a hard-boiled newspaperman.
他觉得自己只是个冷峻老练的报人。

23
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:11,870
Greeley was interested in ideas and in what was happening to the country.
格里利感兴趣的是各种思想，以及这个国家正在经历的一切。

24
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:15,320
Bennett was only interested in his newspaper.
贝内特感兴趣的只有他的报纸。

25
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:20,090
He wanted to find out what the news was, what people wanted to read.
他想弄清楚新闻是什么，人们想读的是什么。

26
00:03:20,890 --> 00:03:22,900
And when he found out he gave it to them.
而他一弄清楚，就把这些提供给人们。

27
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:28,590
As different as Bennett and Greeley were from each other, they were also curiously alike.
贝内特和格里利尽管彼此截然不同，却也出奇地相似。

28
00:03:29,390 --> 00:03:36,070
Both stood outside the circle of polite society, even when they became prosperous, and in Bennett’s case, wealthy.
即便后来两人都已发达——就贝内特而言，更是成了富豪——他们也依然游离于上流社会的圈子之外。

29
00:03:36,870 --> 00:03:38,740
Both were incurable eccentrics.
两人都是无可救药的怪人。

30
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:41,020
Neither was a gentleman.
两人都不是绅士。

31
00:03:41,820 --> 00:03:44,830
Neither conjured up the picture of a successful editor.
两人都不会让人联想到成功编辑的形象。

32
00:03:45,630 --> 00:03:49,210
Greeley was unkempt, always looking like an unmade bed.
格里利不修边幅，看上去总像一张没整理过的床铺。

33
00:03:50,010 --> 00:04:02,650
Even when he was nationally famous in the 1850s he resembled a clerk in a third-rate brokerage house, with slips of paper—marked-up proofs perhaps—hanging out of his pockets or stuck in his hat.
即便在19世纪50年代已经名满全国，他看上去仍像三流经纪行里的小职员，一张张纸条（也许是批改过的校样）不是从衣袋里耷拉出来，就是插在帽子上。

34
00:04:03,450 --> 00:04:08,310
He became fat, was always nearsighted, always peering over spectacles.
他后来发了福，一向近视，总是从眼镜上方眯眼往外瞧。

35
00:04:09,110 --> 00:04:11,780
He spoke in a piping, high-pitched whine.
他说起话来尖声细气，调门很高，像在哀号。

36
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:18,730
Not a few people suggested that he looked exactly like the illustrations of Charles Dickens’s Mr. Pickwick.
不少人说，他看起来跟查尔斯·狄更斯笔下匹克威克先生的插图一模一样。

37
00:04:19,530 --> 00:04:28,450
Greeley provided a humorous description of himself, written under the pretense that it had been the work of his long-time adversary James Fenimore Cooper.
格里利对自己作过一番幽默的描述，假称是出自他的多年宿敌詹姆斯·费尼莫尔·库珀之手。

38
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:39,660
The editor was, according to this little pastiche, a half-bald, long-legged, slouching individual “so rocking in gait that he walks down both sides of the street at once.”
按这篇小小仿作的说法，这位编辑是个半秃顶、长腿、弓腰驼背的人，“走起路来左摇右晃，竟能同时走在街道的两侧”。

39
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:44,380
The appearance of Bennett was somewhat different but hardly more reassuring.
贝内特的外表有些不同，但也很难说更让人放心。

40
00:04:45,180 --> 00:04:52,490
A shrewd, wiry Scotsman, who seemed to repel intimacy, Bennett looked around at the world with a squinty glare of suspicion.
贝内特是个精明、瘦而结实的苏格兰人，似乎排斥与人亲近；他眯着眼，带着怀疑的神色怒视着周围的世界。

41
00:04:53,290 --> 00:04:55,150
His eyes did not focus right.
他的眼睛无法正常聚焦。

42
00:04:55,950 --> 00:04:59,820
They seemed to fix themselves on nothing and everything at the same time.
那双眼睛似乎什么都没盯着，同时又什么都在盯着。

43
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:03,890
He was as solitary as an oyster, the classic loner.
他像牡蛎一样孤僻，是个典型的独行侠。

44
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:13,400
He seldom made close friendships and few people trusted him, although nobody who had dealings with him, however brief, doubted his abilities.
他很少与人深交，也很少有人信任他，不过凡是同他打过交道的人，无论交往多么短暂，都没有怀疑过他的能力。

45
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:25,020
He, too, could have come out of a book of Dickensian eccentrics, although perhaps Ebenezer Scrooge or Thomas Gradgrind comes to mind rather than the kindly old Mr. Pickwick.
他也满可以是从一本狄更斯式怪人录里走出来的人物，不过让人想到的或许是埃比尼泽·斯克鲁奇或托马斯·葛擂硬，而不是和蔼的老匹克威克先生。

46
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:41,060
Greeley was laughed at but admired; Bennett was seldom laughed at but never admired; on the other hand, he had a hard professional competence and an encyclopedic knowledge of his adopted country, an in-depth learning uncorrupted by vague idealisms.
格里利受人嘲笑，却也受人钦佩；贝内特很少受人嘲笑，却从未受人钦佩；但另一方面，他有着过硬的专业能力，对自己移居的这个国家有着百科全书般的了解，这是一种未受空泛理想主义侵蚀的深厚学识。

47
00:05:41,860 --> 00:05:46,280
All of this perfectly suited him for the journalism of this confusing age.
凭着这一切，他在那个纷乱的时代从事新闻业，再合适不过。

48
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:53,440
Both Greeley and Bennett had served long, humiliating and disappointing apprenticeships in the newspaper business.
格里利和贝内特都曾在报业度过漫长、屈辱而又令人失望的学徒生涯。

49
00:05:54,240 --> 00:06:05,100
They took a long time getting to the top, the only reward for the long years of waiting being that when they had their own newspapers, both knew what they wanted and tenaciously set about getting it.
他们花了很长时间才登上顶峰，多年等待换来的唯一回报是：等到有了自己的报纸，两人都清楚自己想要什么，并锲而不舍地着手去争取。

50
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:23,110
Bennett, older by sixteen years, found solid commercial success first, but with no one to offer a word of encouragement, whereas when Greeley founded the Tribune in 1841 he had the strong support of the Whig party and had already had a short period of modest success as an editor.
贝内特比格里利年长十六岁，率先在商业上取得了稳固的成功，却没有任何人给过他一句鼓励；而格里利1841年创办《论坛报》时，有辉格党的大力支持，此前做编辑时也已小有成就，只是为时不长。

51
00:06:23,910 --> 00:06:32,300
When Bennett started up the Herald in 1835 in a dingy cellar room at 20 Wall Street he had no one behind him except himself.
1835年，贝内特在华尔街20号一间昏暗的地下室里创办《先驱报》时，除了他自己，背后没有任何人支持。

52
00:06:33,100 --> 00:06:35,510
Fortunately, this turned out to be quite enough.
幸运的是，结果证明这就已经完全足够了。
